Here's your first C++ program:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    cout << "Hello, World!" << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

Let me explain each part:
• '#include <iostream>' - Includes input/output stream library
• 'using namespace std;' - Lets us use cout without std:: prefix
• 'int main()' - Main function where program starts (returns integer)
• 'cout <<' - Outputs text to console (like System.out.println in Java)
• 'endl' - Ends the line (like \n)
• 'return 0;' - Tells system program ended successfully
• Semicolons end each statement in C++

C++ requires explicit type declarations:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    // Different variable types
    int age = 25;                    // Integer
    double price = 19.99;            // Double precision decimal
    char grade = 'A';                // Single character
    string name = "Alex";            // String (needs #include <string>)
    bool isStudent = true;           // Boolean (true/false)

    // Output variables
    cout << "Name: " << name << endl;
    cout << "Age: " << age << endl;
    cout << "Price: $" << price << endl;
    cout << "Grade: " << grade << endl;
    cout << "Student: " << isStudent << endl;  // 1 for true, 0 for false

    return 0;
}
```

