Getting user input uses 'cin' (console input):

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    string name;
    int age;

    cout << "What's your name? ";
    getline(cin, name);  // getline for strings with spaces

    cout << "How old are you? ";
    cin >> age;          // cin >> for numbers

    cout << "Hello " << name << "!" << endl;
    cout << "You are " << age << " years old." << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

Key differences:
• 'cin >>' for simple input (stops at spaces)
• 'getline(cin, variable)' for full lines with spaces
• Must declare variable types first

